{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789965431,"nonce":"ofCaP6tW1c6T0kdRs/446D2AA6U=","title":"Python: map and filter are eager lists, not lazy iterators","message":"`map(f, xs)` and `filter(f, xs)` are lowered as list comprehensions\n(sugar.rs), so every element is computed at the call. CPython computes\nan element when the iterator is read, which differs where the consumer\nstops early or where the whole result is never read:\n\n- `any(map(f, xs))`, `all(...)` and `for x in map(f, xs): ... break`\n  call `f` on every element here; CPython stops at the first that\n  decides. Observable when `f` prints, counts or raises.\n- `m = map(int, [\"1\", \"z\"])` raises here at the assignment; CPython\n  raises at the first `next(m)`, so a program that never reads `m`\n  past the good element differs, and one that catches around the read\n  rather than the call misses the exception.\n- `map` and `filter` objects print as lists and support `len`, where\n  CPython's do not.\n\nThe same holds for `zip` and `enumerate` fed anywhere but a for loop.\nA fix is the fusion in a253f9f (lower the consumer as the loop, so a\nbreak or a deciding element ends it), plus a fiber-backed iterator for\na map or filter value that escapes.\n\nconformance/iteration/map_raises.py states the eager reading it pins.","files":null}]}